# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL in the default (bridge) mode. JARVIS runs
# perfectly with an empty file — the bridge authenticates off your Claude Code
# login, and voice input and output both fall back to the browser's own speech.
# Copy this to .env.local, and add only what you actually want to change.
#
# The one line worth adding is ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, down in the BRIDGE section:
# it gives you a much better voice and Scribe transcription. The free tier is
# plenty for a demo. Never commit a real key.
#
# Two audiences read these variables, both from .env.local:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* and
#     ELEVENLABS_API_KEY variables. Anything already set in the shell that runs
#     `npm start` wins over the file. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is refused from the file.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* variables.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser.


# ===================================================================
# NAME — read by both the bridge and the page
# ===================================================================
# What he is called. Renames the wake word ("Hey Friday"), the wordmark, the
# tab title, the transcript and how he refers to himself. Default Jarvis.
# JARVIS_NAME=Friday

# Other ways speech recognition hears the name, comma separated. Press D while
# talking to see exactly what it hears, and add anything it gets wrong.
# JARVIS_WAKE_ALIASES=fridey,frida

# The line under the wordmark. Default: the Jarvis backronym for Jarvis,
# nothing for any other name.
# JARVIS_TAGLINE=


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — read from the shell that runs `npm run bridge`
# ===================================================================
# These are shown with their defaults. Leave them unset to accept the default.

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Address the bridge binds to. Default 127.0.0.1, so only this Mac can reach
# it. Setting 0.0.0.0 exposes the agent (mail, browser, files) to your network,
# where the origin check alone cannot keep a non-browser client out.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# The claude.ai connectors JARVIS may use, by name as the startup log lists
# them. Unset, every connector on your account loads. Set, the rest are hidden
# from him and any call to them is refused, which keeps trading, payment and
# hosting connectors out of reach of an instruction planted in an email or web
# page. Local MCP servers and Protective are not connectors and are unaffected.
# The brief, alerts and commitment scan read from the ones in this example.
# JARVIS_CONNECTORS=Gmail, Google Calendar, Microsoft 365, Granola, Atlassian Rovo, Atlassian Rovo (2)

# The model the bridge asks Claude Code to run. Default claude-opus-5.
# JARVIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Reasoning effort. Default high.
# JARVIS_EFFORT=high

# Short, simple turns go to a faster, cheaper model; research, writing,
# analysis, planning and briefings stay on JARVIS_MODEL. Set JARVIS_ROUTING=off
# to send everything to JARVIS_MODEL.
# JARVIS_FAST_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# JARVIS_FAST_EFFORT=low
# JARVIS_ROUTING=on
# The cost of routing: each model keeps its own prompt cache, so the first
# turn on the other model in a burst of conversation re-sends everything at the
# cache-write price — measured at about $0.22 per 22k tokens on Opus, $0.14 on
# Sonnet, against $0.01 for a cached turn. Routing buys the right model and
# effort for each question at roughly one extra full write per burst. One
# model throughout (JARVIS_ROUTING=off, with JARVIS_MODEL / JARVIS_EFFORT set to
# taste) avoids it. Changing only the effort on one model still rewrites the
# conversation part, so it saves little. Each turn's log line shows its cache
# reads and writes.

# Proactive alerts: a heads-up before meetings and for mail that needs you.
# A read-only watcher on the quick model checks while a page is open, within
# working hours. JARVIS_ALERTS=off disables it; JARVIS_ALERT_MAIL_MIN=0 keeps
# meeting alerts only.
# JARVIS_ALERTS=on
# JARVIS_ALERT_LEAD_MIN=10
# JARVIS_ALERT_CAL_MIN=20
# JARVIS_ALERT_MAIL_MIN=15
# JARVIS_ALERT_HOURS=8-19
# JARVIS_ALERT_WEEKENDS=off

# Automatic updates (with `npm run autostart:install` on a Mac): whatever is
# merged to main is pulled and applied once he has been quiet this many minutes.
# JARVIS_AUTO_UPDATE=off turns it off.
# JARVIS_AUTO_UPDATE=on
# JARVIS_UPDATE_IDLE_MIN=15

# Conversations survive a page reload for this many hours after the last turn.
# JARVIS_RESUME=off starts every page load fresh. Lasting notes ("remember that
# …") live in JARVIS_HOME/memory.md — plain Markdown, edit it freely.
# JARVIS_RESUME=on
# JARVIS_RESUME_HOURS=12
# A conversation past this many messages starts fresh on the next page load,
# with its last few exchanges carried over; the whole of a resumed
# conversation is sent with every question. 0 turns the cap off.
# JARVIS_RESUME_MAX=120
# JARVIS_HOME=~/.jarvis

# The conversation history (H on the page): every question, answer and alert,
# one file a day in JARVIS_HOME/transcripts, on this Mac only. Kept this many
# days; JARVIS_HISTORY=off keeps nothing.
# JARVIS_HISTORY=on
# JARVIS_HISTORY_DAYS=30

# Actions that change something (send, reply, create an event, share a file)
# are put to you first: a card, "Shall I proceed?", a yes or no, then four
# seconds to undo. Set JARVIS_CONFIRM=off to refuse them outright instead.
# JARVIS_CONFIRM=on

# Run effectful tools WITHOUT asking, shell and file changes included. Off by
# default. `npm run bridge:writes` sets this to 1 for you — read the decideTool
# gate in bridge/server.mjs first.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Allow tools that move money: placing or cancelling orders, trades, payments,
# invoices, transfers. Each one is still confirmed with you. Reading balances
# and positions never needs it.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_MONEY=0

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# Extra directories the bridge's /file endpoint may serve from, comma separated.
# Your home directory and the system temp directory are always included; this is
# for anything outside them, such as an external drive of footage. Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=

# ElevenLabs voice id for the bridge's own speech endpoint. Default: George.
# JARVIS_VOICE_ID=JBFqnCBsd6RMkjVDRZzb

# Your claude.ai connectors (Gmail, Google Calendar, Drive, …) load with your
# Claude Code login. Set this to 0 in the shell to run without them.
# ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS=1

# ElevenLabs API key. Optional. With it, JARVIS speaks in the ElevenLabs voice
# and transcribes with ElevenLabs Scribe; without it, everything still works on
# the browser's own speech. If you already have an elevenlabs MCP server in
# ~/.claude.json, the bridge reads the key from there and you can leave this
# blank. Default: none.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Which brain the browser talks to. Default bridge.
#   bridge — talk to the local bridge (recommended). No API key, and every MCP
#            server in your Claude Code config is available.
#   direct — the browser calls the Claude API itself. Needs VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# VITE_BACKEND=bridge

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine, used when no ElevenLabs voice is active.
# Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, slower to start.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Prefer the ElevenLabs voice from the browser when a key is available.
# Default false.
# VITE_USE_ELEVENLABS=false

# After an answer he keeps listening this many seconds for a follow-up without
# "hey Jarvis". 0 = every question starts with his name (best in a busy room).
# VITE_FOLLOW_UP_SECONDS=6

# On-device wake word. On by default: "hey Jarvis" is recognised in the page by
# openWakeWord, so nothing said while he is asleep leaves this machine. The
# model is for personal, non-commercial use (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).
# Higher sensitivity catches more, and false-triggers more. 0-1.
# VITE_WAKE_SENSITIVITY=0.5
# For a JARVIS_NAME other than Jarvis: an openWakeWord model placed in public/.
# VITE_WAKE_MODEL=/my-name.onnx
# Optional: Picovoice Porcupine instead (plain "Jarvis", commercial use allowed).
# Needs an AccessKey from console.picovoice.ai; falls back to openWakeWord.
# VITE_PICOVOICE_ACCESS_KEY=
# VITE_WAKE_KEYWORD_FILE=/my-name.ppn
# speech = turn the on-device wake word off and search transcripts instead.
# VITE_WAKE_ENGINE=

# Anthropic API key — DIRECT MODE ONLY. Required if and only if
# VITE_BACKEND=direct, and it ships to the browser, so use it only for a local
# demo. The default bridge mode never reads it. Default: none.
# VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Protective mailbox, calendar and To Do via Power Automate: a file holding
# the daily briefing's flow links (never commit it). Default ~/.jarvis/power-automate.json.
# JARVIS_PA_ENDPOINTS=/path/to/pa_endpoints.json
# The morning brief: offered the first time he is opened in this window.
# JARVIS_BRIEF=on
# JARVIS_BRIEF_HOURS=5-11
# JARVIS_BRIEF_MAX_AGE_MIN=90
# Meeting prep before each heads-up.
# JARVIS_MEETING_PREP=on
# End-of-day wrap-up, offered once in this window on weekdays.
# JARVIS_WRAP=on
# JARVIS_WRAP_HOURS=17-20
# The promise ledger: scans for new promises every N hours, and nudges.
# JARVIS_COMMITMENTS=on
# JARVIS_COMMIT_SCAN_HOURS=4
# Promises others made that are never seen kept are let go after this many
# days (or 14 days past their date). Your own are never retired. 0 = never.
# JARVIS_COMMIT_EXPIRE_DAYS=21
# Focus: whose mail gets through (comma separated), and whether meeting
# heads-ups are held too.
# JARVIS_FOCUS_VIPS=
# JARVIS_FOCUS_HOLD_MEETINGS=off
# Portfolio pulse: Jira projects, dashboards on this Mac (; separated), how
# often the watcher checks for blocked work and slipping sprints (0 = off).
# JARVIS_JIRA_PROJECTS=NI,RPT
# JARVIS_PORTFOLIO_FILES=
# JARVIS_PORTFOLIO_ALERT_MIN=60
# JARVIS_SPRINT_SLIP_PCT=25
# Where ticket keys link to. Learned from the Jira connector if unset.
# JARVIS_JIRA_URL=https://yoursite.atlassian.net
# JARVIS_ADO_URL=https://yourorg.visualstudio.com
# The Friday weekly review, offered once in this window.
# JARVIS_REVIEW=on
# JARVIS_REVIEW_HOURS=14-18
